[# Section 1b — choosing a ring: the audience router. The four reader
   paths ruled in the #826 proposal §4, plus the task router (moved here
   from the rings section, which now hands off). Each path terminates in
   something the reader can DO. Authored 2026-08-19 for #826. #]

[section id=choosing group='Start here' n='1b' title='Choosing a ring'

  [child id=choose-task n=1b.1 title='Start from what you want to do'
    [intro
      """Take the smallest ring that answers your question — you can
         always move outward later, and nothing you wrote against a
         lower ring changes when you do, because the lower ring is
         unchanged by the higher one (see [[rings]] for why that holds
         structurally)."""]

    [table
      [tr [th 'If you want to…'] [th 'You need']]
      [tr [td 'read, write, or convert CX documents'] [td 'Ring 0']]
      [tr [td 'give data a stable identity, or diff two versions'] [td 'Ring 0']]
      [tr [td 'validate against a schema'] [td 'Ring 0']]
      [tr [td 'embed CX somewhere that must not run code'] [td 'Ring 0']]
      [tr [td 'query, transform, or generate with programs'] [td 'Ring 1']]
      [tr [td 'run a program with controlled access to files or the network'] [td 'Ring 1']]
      [tr [td 'store data, keep history, or serve it'] [td 'Ring 2']]
      [tr [td 'host an XAP feature'] [td 'Ring 2']]
      [tr [td 'publish, discover, or install a feature'] [td 'Ring 3']]
      [tr [td 'call CX from Go, Python, Rust, or V'] [td 'Ring 3']]]

    [body
      """If the table already answered you, jump to that ring's group in
         the sidebar — each ring's pages open with what the ring is and
         end with the packs it gives you. If you recognize yourself in
         one of the four readers below, follow the path — each is
         ordered, each stop earns the next, and each ends in something
         you can do."""]]

  [child id=choose-evaluator n=1b.2 title='The first-time evaluator'
    [body
      """*\"What is this, and is it for me?\"* — answerable without
         meeting a single directive:"""]
    [list
      [item """[[intro]] — what CX is and what problem it refuses to
              have."""]
      [item """[[rings]] — the shape of the system: four rings, one
              import direction."""]
      [item """[[quickstart]] — the first five minutes; it runs inside
              Ring 0 before it shows you anything that executes."""]
      [item """[[comparison]] — CX against JSON, YAML, XML, Protocol
              Buffers, and friends, in both directions."""]]
    [body
      """You are done when you can say what CX would replace in your
         stack and which ring that replacement lives in. If the answer
         is \"nothing\", the comparison page will have told you that
         honestly."""]]

  [child id=choose-data-adopter n=1b.3 title='The data-format adopter'
    [body
      """You want the format — parse, validate, convert, hash, diff —
         and you never intend to run a program. Ring 0 is a complete
         product for you, and this path never asks you to understand
         the evaluator:"""]
    [list
      [item """[[data]] — the data language end to end: elements,
              scalars, collections, anchors."""]
      [item """[[value-model]] — one value, one address: what identity
              buys you and what rides in the three lanes."""]
      [item """[[surfaces]] — projections to and from JSON, XML, YAML,
              TOML, Markdown, and the binary forms."""]
      [item """[[identity]] — canonical form and hashing, the mechanics
              under the promises."""]
      [item """[[schema-evolution]] — validation, computable
              compatibility, and what happens when schemas change."""]
      [item """[[tour-data]] — fifty worked data recipes; [[analytics]]
              for the columnar and bridge story."""]]
    [body
      """You are done when `cx fmt`, `cx validate`, `cx hash`, and
         `cx diff` are in your build, and the Ring-0 pack index (on the
         ring's pages) tells you exactly which library surface you may
         lean on — and that `http` is not in it."""]]

  [child id=choose-app-author n=1b.4 title='The application author'
    [body
      """You want to build something that runs — programs first, then a
         feature on the platform:"""]
    [list
      [item """[[quickstart]] — the Ring-1 half: your first programs."""]
      [item """[[code]] — the code language: directives, CXPath,
              patterns, errors as values."""]
      [item """[[capabilities]] — deny-by-default and the grant flags;
              read this before your first `--allow-*`."""]
      [item """[[computation-identity]] and [[commands-effects]] — what
              makes results cacheable and effects auditable."""]
      [item """[[tour-programs]] — fifty worked program recipes."""]
      [item """[[store]] and [[xap-distribution]] — when your program
              becomes a feature others install: authoring, hosting, and
              distribution."""]]
    [body
      """The end-to-end worked story is the ORIEL reference surface — a
         storefront with departments, baskets, checkout, and three
         renderers, built entirely from declarations. Its developer
         guide is `docs/dev/oriel-guide.md` (\"build a surface the ORIEL
         way\"), and the task-oriented developer set under `docs/dev/`
         walks authoring, consuming, and distributing features."""]]

  [child id=choose-operator n=1b.5 title='The platform operator'
    [body
      """You run the thing: stores, journals, services, upgrades."""]
    [list
      [item """[[store]] — the store platform: content addressing,
              refs, journals, folds."""]
      [item """[[bitemporal]], [[consistency]], [[event-evolution]] —
              the three contracts you operate under: two time axes,
              declared guarantees, and reading old events under new
              vocabulary."""]
      [item """[[operations]] — daemons, health, recovery, security
              posture; the operator reading order continues in
              `docs/dev/` (store service, management, security)."""]
      [item """[[xap-distribution]] — the distribution machinery your
              developers will ask you to operate."""]]
    [body
      """You are done when you can answer, without opening a runbook:
         where is history kept (one journal, append-only), what does a
         correction do to an invoice already sent (nothing —
         [[bitemporal]]), and what happens when a consumer asks for a
         guarantee a replica cannot give (a loud refusal naming the
         token — [[consistency]])."""]]
]
